1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,564
Sweetheart, in December of nineteen ninety-one,

2
00:00:03,564 --> 00:00:05,839
the mailman put a letter in my

3
00:00:05,839 --> 00:00:06,977
box by mistake.

4
00:00:07,157 --> 00:00:09,390
It was addressed in crayon,

5
00:00:09,390 --> 00:00:10,630
to Santa Claus,

6
00:00:10,630 --> 00:00:13,276
North Pole, and it had no stamp.

7
00:00:13,456 --> 00:00:15,785
The little girl who wrote it didn't

8
00:00:15,785 --> 00:00:17,449
ask for a doll or a bike.

9
00:00:17,629 --> 00:00:20,150
She asked Santa for a winter coat

10
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:21,295
for her mother,

11
00:00:21,295 --> 00:00:23,663
and for her daddy to stop being

12
00:00:23,663 --> 00:00:23,968
sad.

13
00:00:24,148 --> 00:00:26,854
I've never thrown that letter away.

14
00:00:27,034 --> 00:00:28,322
And I answered it.

15
00:00:28,673 --> 00:00:31,080
Tonight I'm going to tell you what

16
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:32,284
that letter said,

17
00:00:32,284 --> 00:00:34,479
how I found the little girl who

18
00:00:34,479 --> 00:00:36,604
wrote it, and what she sent me

19
00:00:36,604 --> 00:00:39,224
in the mail twenty-eight years later,

20
00:00:39,224 --> 00:00:41,490
in a box with no return address.

21
00:00:41,670 --> 00:00:42,560
Stay with me,

22
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:45,297
honey, because her father's part of this

23
00:00:45,297 --> 00:00:47,556
story is the one that still makes

24
00:00:47,556 --> 00:00:49,472
grown men cry when I tell it

25
00:00:49,472 --> 00:00:50,157
at church.

26
00:00:50,507 --> 00:00:51,858
It was a Tuesday,

27
00:00:51,858 --> 00:00:53,606
the tenth of December.

28
00:00:53,786 --> 00:00:55,819
I was bringing in the mail,

29
00:00:55,819 --> 00:00:57,777
bills and Christmas cards,

30
00:00:57,777 --> 00:00:59,057
and there it was,

31
00:00:59,057 --> 00:01:02,068
a folded piece of school notebook paper,

32
00:01:02,068 --> 00:01:03,875
tucked in with the rest.

33
00:01:04,055 --> 00:01:05,637
On the outside,

34
00:01:05,637 --> 00:01:07,112
in red crayon,

35
00:01:07,112 --> 00:01:09,537
it said, To Santa Clos,

36
00:01:09,537 --> 00:01:10,696
North Pole.

37
00:01:10,876 --> 00:01:13,442
Somebody had dropped it in a mailbox

38
00:01:13,442 --> 00:01:14,440
with no stamp,

39
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:16,791
and the post office had just sort

40
00:01:16,791 --> 00:01:19,214
of passed it along until it landed

41
00:01:19,214 --> 00:01:19,642
on me.

42
00:01:19,992 --> 00:01:21,307
I opened it,

43
00:01:21,307 --> 00:01:21,965
honey.

44
00:01:22,145 --> 00:01:24,177
I know I shouldn't have.

45
00:01:24,357 --> 00:01:26,946
It said, Dear Santa.

46
00:01:27,126 --> 00:01:28,856
My name is Emily.

47
00:01:29,036 --> 00:01:30,197
I am seven.

48
00:01:30,377 --> 00:01:31,933
I have been good.

49
00:01:32,113 --> 00:01:35,015
I don't need any toys this year.

50
00:01:35,195 --> 00:01:37,463
Please bring my mom a winter coat

51
00:01:37,463 --> 00:01:39,594
because hers has a hole and she

52
00:01:39,594 --> 00:01:41,244
is cold at the bus stop.

53
00:01:41,424 --> 00:01:43,941
And please make my daddy not sad

54
00:01:43,941 --> 00:01:44,570
anymore.

55
00:01:44,750 --> 00:01:46,601
He lost his job at the plant

56
00:01:46,601 --> 00:01:48,187
and he sits in the dark.

57
00:01:48,367 --> 00:01:50,143
Love, Emily.

58
00:01:50,323 --> 00:01:51,182
P.

59
00:01:51,362 --> 00:01:52,210
S.

60
00:01:52,390 --> 00:01:54,187
We don't have a chimney,

61
00:01:54,187 --> 00:01:55,385
so use the door.

62
00:01:55,736 --> 00:01:57,834
I sat right down on my porch

63
00:01:57,834 --> 00:01:59,933
step in the cold and read it

64
00:01:59,933 --> 00:02:00,832
three times.

65
00:02:01,012 --> 00:02:02,684
We don't have a chimney,

66
00:02:02,684 --> 00:02:03,799
so use the door.

67
00:02:03,979 --> 00:02:06,798
Harold came home and found me there,

68
00:02:06,798 --> 00:02:08,286
with my cheeks wet.

69
00:02:08,466 --> 00:02:09,269
He read it,

70
00:02:09,269 --> 00:02:11,241
and he didn't say anything.

71
00:02:11,421 --> 00:02:14,262
He just went inside and came back

72
00:02:14,262 --> 00:02:16,500
with his coat on and said,

73
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:18,050
all right, Ruthie.

74
00:02:18,230 --> 00:02:20,122
How are we going to find her?

75
00:02:20,302 --> 00:02:22,330
In a minute I'll tell you the

76
00:02:22,330 --> 00:02:23,727
clue that found her.

77
00:02:24,078 --> 00:02:25,989
There was no last name,

78
00:02:25,989 --> 00:02:28,234
sweetheart, and no address.

79
00:02:28,414 --> 00:02:29,942
Just Emily, seven,

80
00:02:29,942 --> 00:02:32,488
and a daddy who'd lost his job

81
00:02:32,488 --> 00:02:33,592
at the plant.

82
00:02:33,772 --> 00:02:35,865
But in nineteen ninety-one,

83
00:02:35,865 --> 00:02:38,657
only one plant in Mansfield had laid

84
00:02:38,657 --> 00:02:40,285
people off that fall.

85
00:02:40,465 --> 00:02:43,493
The big appliance plant on Fifth Street

86
00:02:43,493 --> 00:02:45,822
had let go of four hundred men

87
00:02:45,822 --> 00:02:46,676
in October.

88
00:02:46,856 --> 00:02:48,877
Harold had friends there.

89
00:02:49,057 --> 00:02:51,803
So Harold started asking around at the

90
00:02:51,803 --> 00:02:52,598
union hall.

91
00:02:52,948 --> 00:02:55,037
And the second clue was in the

92
00:02:55,037 --> 00:02:56,500
letter itself, honey.

93
00:02:56,680 --> 00:02:57,736
On the back,

94
00:02:57,736 --> 00:03:00,024
Emily had drawn a picture.

95
00:03:00,204 --> 00:03:01,656
A little house,

96
00:03:01,656 --> 00:03:02,915
a mom, a dad,

97
00:03:02,915 --> 00:03:03,980
and a girl,

98
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:05,335
all in crayon.

99
00:03:05,515 --> 00:03:07,207
And next to the house,

100
00:03:07,207 --> 00:03:09,590
she'd drawn a tall tower with a

101
00:03:09,590 --> 00:03:10,666
big round top,

102
00:03:10,666 --> 00:03:11,820
colored silver.

103
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,463
I knew that tower.

104
00:03:13,643 --> 00:03:15,891
It was the water tower on the

105
00:03:15,891 --> 00:03:17,287
north end of town,

106
00:03:17,287 --> 00:03:19,924
by the trailer park on Leppo Road.

107
00:03:20,274 --> 00:03:23,112
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

108
00:03:23,112 --> 00:03:25,302
tap the like button for me,

109
00:03:25,302 --> 00:03:26,195
sweetheart.

110
00:03:26,375 --> 00:03:29,916
It helps more people find these stories.

111
00:03:30,096 --> 00:03:31,270
Two days later,

112
00:03:31,270 --> 00:03:33,462
a man at the union hall told

113
00:03:33,462 --> 00:03:36,594
Harold about a fellow named Dale Pruett,

114
00:03:36,594 --> 00:03:39,256
who'd been laid off in October and

115
00:03:39,256 --> 00:03:41,918
lived in the trailer park on Leppo

116
00:03:41,918 --> 00:03:43,406
Road with his wife,

117
00:03:43,406 --> 00:03:45,676
Karen, and their little girl.

118
00:03:45,856 --> 00:03:48,445
And their little girl's name was Emily.

119
00:03:48,796 --> 00:03:52,017
Harold drove out there the next afternoon,

120
00:03:52,017 --> 00:03:53,014
just to look,

121
00:03:53,014 --> 00:03:53,474
honey.

122
00:03:53,654 --> 00:03:55,958
He parked by the water tower and

123
00:03:55,958 --> 00:03:56,534
watched.

124
00:03:56,714 --> 00:03:58,480
And he saw a woman come out

125
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:00,509
of a little blue trailer at the

126
00:04:00,509 --> 00:04:02,080
end of the row in a thin

127
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:03,977
brown coat with a tear at the

128
00:04:03,977 --> 00:04:06,006
elbow, and walk to the bus stop

129
00:04:06,006 --> 00:04:06,791
in the wind.

130
00:04:06,971 --> 00:04:09,282
She stood there with her arms wrapped

131
00:04:09,282 --> 00:04:11,406
around herself for twenty minutes.

132
00:04:11,586 --> 00:04:14,015
Harold came home and said,

133
00:04:14,015 --> 00:04:15,603
Ruthie, it's her.

134
00:04:15,783 --> 00:04:17,873
And that coat has a hole in

135
00:04:17,873 --> 00:04:18,105
it.

136
00:04:18,456 --> 00:04:20,964
Now, here's the thing,

137
00:04:20,964 --> 00:04:21,648
honey.

138
00:04:21,828 --> 00:04:23,825
Dale was a proud man.

139
00:04:24,005 --> 00:04:25,892
Harold knew men like him,

140
00:04:25,892 --> 00:04:28,910
men who'd worked since they were sixteen

141
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:31,703
and never asked anybody for anything.

142
00:04:31,883 --> 00:04:34,405
If two strangers showed up at his

143
00:04:34,405 --> 00:04:35,781
door with charity,

144
00:04:35,781 --> 00:04:37,769
he'd slam it in our faces.

145
00:04:37,949 --> 00:04:39,509
Harold said, Ruthie,

146
00:04:39,509 --> 00:04:41,537
a man who sits in the dark

147
00:04:41,537 --> 00:04:42,941
doesn't want pity.

148
00:04:43,121 --> 00:04:44,886
He wants to be needed.

149
00:04:45,066 --> 00:04:47,934
And that's when Harold got his idea.

150
00:04:48,283 --> 00:04:49,714
Harold had a friend,

151
00:04:49,714 --> 00:04:52,433
George Hill, who owned a small heating

152
00:04:52,433 --> 00:04:54,007
and plumbing business,

153
00:04:54,007 --> 00:04:57,227
and George was always complaining he couldn't

154
00:04:57,227 --> 00:04:59,373
find good men who'd show up on

155
00:04:59,373 --> 00:04:59,731
time.

156
00:04:59,911 --> 00:05:02,548
So Harold went to George and said,

157
00:05:02,548 --> 00:05:03,556
I know a man.

158
00:05:03,736 --> 00:05:07,185
Worked at the appliance plant eighteen years.

159
00:05:07,365 --> 00:05:08,886
Never missed a day.

160
00:05:09,066 --> 00:05:11,724
George said, send him over.

161
00:05:11,904 --> 00:05:13,865
And Harold said,

162
00:05:13,865 --> 00:05:15,213
no, George.

163
00:05:15,393 --> 00:05:16,926
You go to him.

164
00:05:17,106 --> 00:05:19,230
And you tell him you heard he

165
00:05:19,230 --> 00:05:20,182
was the best.

166
00:05:20,532 --> 00:05:22,297
And then there was the coat.

167
00:05:22,477 --> 00:05:24,852
I went to Lazarus in Columbus and

168
00:05:24,852 --> 00:05:27,586
bought the warmest winter coat I could

169
00:05:27,586 --> 00:05:29,744
find, a long red wool one with

170
00:05:29,744 --> 00:05:31,831
a hood, in the size I guessed

171
00:05:31,831 --> 00:05:33,054
from the drawing.

172
00:05:33,234 --> 00:05:35,437
Which was a silly way to guess

173
00:05:35,437 --> 00:05:36,833
a size, sweetheart.

174
00:05:37,013 --> 00:05:39,124
And I bought a little doll for

175
00:05:39,124 --> 00:05:41,517
Emily, too, because she was seven,

176
00:05:41,517 --> 00:05:43,909
and I didn't care what she'd said.

177
00:05:44,089 --> 00:05:46,748
Every seven-year-old needs a doll.

178
00:05:47,097 --> 00:05:49,605
And I bought groceries too,

179
00:05:49,605 --> 00:05:50,162
honey.

180
00:05:50,342 --> 00:05:51,900
A ham, potatoes,

181
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:53,554
a bag of oranges,

182
00:05:53,554 --> 00:05:55,500
a box of chocolates,

183
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:58,225
and a tin of my own molasses

184
00:05:58,225 --> 00:05:59,004
cookies.

185
00:05:59,184 --> 00:06:00,845
Harold said, Ruth,

186
00:06:00,845 --> 00:06:03,061
Santa doesn't bring ham.

187
00:06:03,241 --> 00:06:04,414
And I said,

188
00:06:04,414 --> 00:06:06,121
this Santa does.

189
00:06:06,301 --> 00:06:08,193
We packed it all in a big

190
00:06:08,193 --> 00:06:10,843
box, and Harold wrapped it in brown

191
00:06:10,843 --> 00:06:12,887
paper, and on top he wrote,

192
00:06:12,887 --> 00:06:15,537
in big block letters so it wouldn't

193
00:06:15,537 --> 00:06:17,429
look like my handwriting,

194
00:06:17,429 --> 00:06:19,095
For the Pruett family.

195
00:06:19,275 --> 00:06:20,738
From the North Pole.

196
00:06:21,087 --> 00:06:22,615
So far, the letter,

197
00:06:22,615 --> 00:06:23,902
the water tower,

198
00:06:23,902 --> 00:06:25,430
and the union hall.

199
00:06:25,610 --> 00:06:26,994
On Christmas Eve,

200
00:06:26,994 --> 00:06:29,029
at nine o'clock at night,

201
00:06:29,029 --> 00:06:31,553
Harold and I drove out to Leppo

202
00:06:31,553 --> 00:06:31,960
Road.

203
00:06:32,140 --> 00:06:34,622
The trailer was dark except for one

204
00:06:34,622 --> 00:06:35,472
little lamp.

205
00:06:35,652 --> 00:06:36,964
We didn't knock.

206
00:06:37,144 --> 00:06:39,300
We left the two boxes on the

207
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:41,533
step, wrapped in brown paper.

208
00:06:41,713 --> 00:06:43,218
On top was a letter,

209
00:06:43,218 --> 00:06:45,400
and I'd written it on my best

210
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:47,809
stationery, with a fountain pen,

211
00:06:47,809 --> 00:06:50,142
in my most careful handwriting.

212
00:06:50,491 --> 00:06:52,906
It said, Dear Emily.

213
00:06:53,086 --> 00:06:54,352
I got your letter.

214
00:06:54,532 --> 00:06:57,459
You're right, you don't have a chimney,

215
00:06:57,459 --> 00:06:58,886
so I used the door.

216
00:06:59,066 --> 00:07:01,279
Here is a coat for your mother,

217
00:07:01,279 --> 00:07:03,206
so she won't be cold at the

218
00:07:03,206 --> 00:07:03,849
bus stop.

219
00:07:04,029 --> 00:07:06,176
And a little something for you,

220
00:07:06,176 --> 00:07:08,669
because good girls who don't ask for

221
00:07:08,669 --> 00:07:11,717
anything for themselves are my very favorite

222
00:07:11,717 --> 00:07:12,063
kind.

223
00:07:12,243 --> 00:07:15,047
Your daddy's sadness is a harder thing

224
00:07:15,047 --> 00:07:15,563
to fix.

225
00:07:15,743 --> 00:07:17,253
But I'm working on it.

226
00:07:17,433 --> 00:07:19,441
Love, Santa.

227
00:07:19,791 --> 00:07:22,614
Then we rang the doorbell and ran,

228
00:07:22,614 --> 00:07:23,112
honey.

229
00:07:23,292 --> 00:07:24,554
Harold was fifty,

230
00:07:24,554 --> 00:07:25,741
with a bad knee,

231
00:07:25,741 --> 00:07:27,375
and I was forty-seven,

232
00:07:27,375 --> 00:07:29,750
and we ran down that gravel lane

233
00:07:29,750 --> 00:07:31,828
in the dark like a couple of

234
00:07:31,828 --> 00:07:34,797
teenagers and ducked behind our own car.

235
00:07:34,977 --> 00:07:37,347
And we watched the door open,

236
00:07:37,347 --> 00:07:40,042
and a little girl in pajamas look

237
00:07:40,042 --> 00:07:41,676
down at those boxes.

238
00:07:41,856 --> 00:07:43,825
And we heard her scream,

239
00:07:43,825 --> 00:07:44,318
Mommy!

240
00:07:44,498 --> 00:07:45,310
Daddy!

241
00:07:45,490 --> 00:07:46,408
He came!

242
00:07:46,588 --> 00:07:47,818
He used the door!

243
00:07:48,168 --> 00:07:49,981
Two days after Christmas,

244
00:07:49,981 --> 00:07:52,592
George Hill drove out to Leppo Road.

245
00:07:52,772 --> 00:07:54,771
He knocked on the door and said

246
00:07:54,771 --> 00:07:56,577
to Dale, I hear you were the

247
00:07:56,577 --> 00:07:58,318
best man on the line at the

248
00:07:58,318 --> 00:07:58,705
plant.

249
00:07:58,885 --> 00:08:00,870
I need a man like that.

250
00:08:01,050 --> 00:08:02,675
Can you start Monday?

251
00:08:02,855 --> 00:08:04,774
Dale stood in that doorway,

252
00:08:04,774 --> 00:08:07,332
honey, and George said he didn't say

253
00:08:07,332 --> 00:08:09,322
anything for a whole minute.

254
00:08:09,502 --> 00:08:12,072
Then he shook George's hand so hard

255
00:08:12,072 --> 00:08:14,494
George's fingers hurt for a week.

256
00:08:14,844 --> 00:08:17,419
Dale never knew it was Harold,

257
00:08:17,419 --> 00:08:18,362
sweetheart.

258
00:08:18,542 --> 00:08:20,272
George never told him.

259
00:08:20,452 --> 00:08:23,120
He thought George Hill had just heard

260
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:24,562
about him somewhere.

261
00:08:24,742 --> 00:08:27,539
He worked for George for twenty-two years

262
00:08:27,539 --> 00:08:29,107
and became his partner.

263
00:08:29,287 --> 00:08:31,025
And when George retired,

264
00:08:31,025 --> 00:08:33,270
Dale bought the whole business.

265
00:08:33,450 --> 00:08:35,249
Pruett and Hill Heating.

266
00:08:35,429 --> 00:08:38,207
You'll still see their trucks all over

267
00:08:38,207 --> 00:08:39,377
Mansfield today.

268
00:08:39,557 --> 00:08:41,599
Every one of them has a little

269
00:08:41,599 --> 00:08:43,028
red star on the door.

270
00:08:43,378 --> 00:08:45,606
And every Christmas after that,

271
00:08:45,606 --> 00:08:48,050
honey, a card came to the trailer,

272
00:08:48,050 --> 00:08:50,422
and later to the little house the

273
00:08:50,422 --> 00:08:52,579
Pruetts bought on Wood Street,

274
00:08:52,579 --> 00:08:53,513
signed Santa.

275
00:08:53,693 --> 00:08:56,202
Every year until Emily was twelve,

276
00:08:56,202 --> 00:08:59,080
in my careful fountain pen handwriting.

277
00:08:59,260 --> 00:09:01,211
One year I sent her a book.

278
00:09:01,391 --> 00:09:03,701
One year, ice skates.

279
00:09:03,881 --> 00:09:05,740
And when she was twelve,

280
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:07,677
I sent her a last letter,

281
00:09:07,677 --> 00:09:10,311
telling her she was old enough now

282
00:09:10,311 --> 00:09:12,635
to be Santa for somebody else.

283
00:09:12,984 --> 00:09:15,406
Harold passed in two thousand eight,

284
00:09:15,406 --> 00:09:17,492
and he never knew how the story

285
00:09:17,492 --> 00:09:17,896
ended.

286
00:09:18,076 --> 00:09:20,894
In December of two thousand nineteen,

287
00:09:20,894 --> 00:09:22,951
a box came in the mail with

288
00:09:22,951 --> 00:09:24,322
no return address.

289
00:09:24,502 --> 00:09:28,138
Inside, honey, was the letter I'd written

290
00:09:28,138 --> 00:09:28,937
as Santa.

291
00:09:29,117 --> 00:09:30,365
On my stationery,

292
00:09:30,365 --> 00:09:31,686
in my handwriting,

293
00:09:31,686 --> 00:09:33,008
yellowed and soft,

294
00:09:33,008 --> 00:09:35,504
folded so many times it was coming

295
00:09:35,504 --> 00:09:37,046
apart at the creases.

296
00:09:37,226 --> 00:09:38,694
And a new letter,

297
00:09:38,694 --> 00:09:39,212
typed.

298
00:09:39,561 --> 00:09:41,767
It said, Dear Mrs.

299
00:09:41,947 --> 00:09:42,644
Miller.

300
00:09:42,824 --> 00:09:45,459
My name is Emily Pruett Walsh.

301
00:09:45,639 --> 00:09:47,276
I'm thirty-five.

302
00:09:47,456 --> 00:09:49,313
My dad died last month,

303
00:09:49,313 --> 00:09:50,927
and in his tool box,

304
00:09:50,927 --> 00:09:53,832
we found this letter from Santa that

305
00:09:53,832 --> 00:09:55,769
I'd lost when I was ten.

306
00:09:55,949 --> 00:09:58,596
He'd kept it for twenty-five years.

307
00:09:58,776 --> 00:10:01,216
And folded inside it was a note

308
00:10:01,216 --> 00:10:02,712
in his handwriting.

309
00:10:02,892 --> 00:10:06,006
It said, Ask Ruth Miller on Maple

310
00:10:06,006 --> 00:10:06,572
Drive.

311
00:10:06,752 --> 00:10:07,855
Her stationery.

312
00:10:08,035 --> 00:10:10,241
Her husband got me the job.

313
00:10:10,421 --> 00:10:11,571
I knew.

314
00:10:11,751 --> 00:10:13,202
I always knew.

315
00:10:13,552 --> 00:10:15,550
Dale had figured it out,

316
00:10:15,550 --> 00:10:16,466
sweetheart.

317
00:10:16,646 --> 00:10:18,329
Years and years ago.

318
00:10:18,509 --> 00:10:21,110
He'd seen my handwriting on a church

319
00:10:21,110 --> 00:10:24,001
potluck sign-up sheet and recognized it.

320
00:10:24,181 --> 00:10:26,274
And he never said one word to

321
00:10:26,274 --> 00:10:28,945
us, because he knew Harold had wanted

322
00:10:28,945 --> 00:10:29,811
it that way.

323
00:10:29,991 --> 00:10:32,310
He let Harold give him his dignity,

324
00:10:32,310 --> 00:10:34,496
and he gave Harold the dignity of

325
00:10:34,496 --> 00:10:36,284
never having to be thanked.

326
00:10:36,464 --> 00:10:37,959
Two proud men,

327
00:10:37,959 --> 00:10:38,600
honey.

328
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:40,905
Neither one ever said a thing.

329
00:10:41,255 --> 00:10:43,870
Emily came to see me that Christmas

330
00:10:43,870 --> 00:10:44,169
Eve.

331
00:10:44,349 --> 00:10:45,940
She's a teacher now,

332
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:47,771
a second-grade teacher,

333
00:10:47,771 --> 00:10:48,726
in Columbus.

334
00:10:48,906 --> 00:10:51,531
And she told me that every December,

335
00:10:51,531 --> 00:10:54,668
she has her second-graders write letters to

336
00:10:54,668 --> 00:10:55,105
Santa.

337
00:10:55,285 --> 00:10:57,375
And then she reads every one.

338
00:10:57,555 --> 00:10:58,692
And every year,

339
00:10:58,692 --> 00:11:00,360
there's one like hers,

340
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:02,407
a child who asks for a coat

341
00:11:02,407 --> 00:11:03,696
for their mother,

342
00:11:03,696 --> 00:11:06,122
or for their daddy to stop being

343
00:11:06,122 --> 00:11:06,425
sad.

344
00:11:06,605 --> 00:11:08,405
And she answers that one.

345
00:11:08,585 --> 00:11:09,966
Using the door.

346
00:11:10,317 --> 00:11:12,158
She brought me a present,

347
00:11:12,158 --> 00:11:12,453
too.

348
00:11:12,633 --> 00:11:14,386
A framed picture.

349
00:11:14,566 --> 00:11:17,136
It was her crayon drawing from nineteen

350
00:11:17,136 --> 00:11:19,904
ninety-one, the house and the water tower,

351
00:11:19,904 --> 00:11:22,540
that she'd found in Dale's toolbox along

352
00:11:22,540 --> 00:11:23,923
with everything else.

353
00:11:24,103 --> 00:11:26,863
And she'd added something with a new

354
00:11:26,863 --> 00:11:28,933
red crayon, very carefully.

355
00:11:29,113 --> 00:11:30,727
A little old couple,

356
00:11:30,727 --> 00:11:32,906
running down a gravel lane,

357
00:11:32,906 --> 00:11:34,036
holding hands.

358
00:11:34,216 --> 00:11:36,004
It's on my kitchen wall,

359
00:11:36,004 --> 00:11:38,314
right next to the refrigerator.

360
00:11:38,664 --> 00:11:41,261
Here's what I want you to remember,

361
00:11:41,261 --> 00:11:41,706
honey.

362
00:11:41,886 --> 00:11:44,686
Sometimes the best way to help somebody

363
00:11:44,686 --> 00:11:46,912
is to make sure they never have

364
00:11:46,912 --> 00:11:48,132
to say thank you.

365
00:11:48,312 --> 00:11:49,389
Give the coat,

366
00:11:49,389 --> 00:11:50,774
and ring the bell,

367
00:11:50,774 --> 00:11:51,389
and run.

368
00:11:51,569 --> 00:11:53,102
Tell me in the comments,

369
00:11:53,102 --> 00:11:55,337
sweetheart, did you ever get a gift

370
00:11:55,337 --> 00:11:57,571
from Santa you could never explain?

371
00:11:57,751 --> 00:12:00,291
There are more of my family's stories

372
00:12:00,291 --> 00:12:02,692
in the playlist right there on your

373
00:12:02,692 --> 00:12:04,820
screen, and I'll see you in the

374
00:12:04,820 --> 00:12:05,437
next one.

375
00:12:05,617 --> 00:12:06,976
Merry Christmas.
